// automations.memql
//
// Deployment-namespace ORCHESTRATION -- the keystone of the DevOps deployment
// bundle (epic #2212, I10 #2224). Per the behavioral-constructs ADR §2: logic
// decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world, AUTOMATIONS ORCHESTRATE.
// This file authors the one automation that ties the bundle together:
// deployEngineCluster sequences the I8 deploy actions (actions.memql) with the
// I9 deploy decisions (logic.memql) and the persisted deployment lifecycle
// (createDeployment / updateDeploymentStatus, dsl/deployment/mutations.memql), one
// write per step. It is the single construct allowed to compose all the layers
// (branch / loop / call logic+query+mutation+action), and it stays
// contract-clean: the logics it calls are pure, each mutation step is one
// write, each action step is one external capability on the cockpit/runner
// surface (I13). It therefore PASSES the call-graph validator
// (test/dslconformance/callgraph_contract_test.go) rather than being baselined.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Deploy event taxonomy
// =============================================================================
//
// One event drives the whole bundle:
//
//   topic    : deploy.requested
//   concept  : v1:cluster:deployment
//   payload  : validated against the automation's `args { }` block below --
//              THE machine-checked emitter/consumer contract (G5, #2367).
//              A missing @required field or an @enum violation makes the
//              automation REFUSE to fire loudly (G1 fire-time validation);
//              field semantics are documented as @description annotations
//              on the args fields themselves.
//
// WHO EMITS IT -- NOBODY, AS OF memql#4550. Read this before concluding
// either that the topic is live or that it is safe to delete.
//
// The primary emitter was the cockpit's `deploy --env=<env>` (I15): `make
// deploy` shelled into it, and its embedded engine runtime loaded the pinned
// deployment bundle, emitted deploy.requested, and ran THIS automation. That
// whole family -- the subcommand, the embedded runtime, scripts/deploy/
// cockpit.sh and the make target -- was removed with the Cockpit's TUI. The
// two other emitters this comment used to name were always prospective: a CI
// release pipeline "can" emit it, and a cron emitter was "future". Neither
// exists.
//
// So this subscriber currently has NO emitter, in this repo or out of it.
// That is a change from what this comment said before, and the change is why
// it is restated rather than trimmed: memql#3614's orphan-topic audit landed
// on this line and was answered with in-repo proof that the topic was live --
// scripts/deploy/cockpit.sh chief among it. Deleting that file made the
// answer false, and a stale "it is not dead, here is why" is worse for the
// next audit than no comment at all.
//
// WHAT IS STILL TRUE. The automation is emitter-agnostic and its phase model
// (authorize / record / clone / build / place / gate / outcome / finalize /
// rollback) is how a deploy is modelled in the graph; the
// `v1:cluster:deployment` timeline is still the record of evidence, and
// component/automations/args_binding.go's []string accommodation -- made FOR
// the cockpit's engineNodeTypes default during the first live deploy
// (memql#2380) -- is still load-bearing for anything that emits this. The DSL
// lifecycle automations (memql#4490) are what decide this bundle's fate; until
// they do, treat it as retained-on-purpose rather than orphaned-by-accident.
// docs/public/operate/deploy-bundle-runbook.md carries the same note, at
// `status: historical`.
//
// TOPIC SHAPE. `deploy.requested` is a RAW application topic, not graph CDC, so
// it carries no concept segment. This trigger used to read
// `@trigger(event="deploy.requested", concept="v1:cluster:deployment",
// partition="*")`; those two kwargs are consumed only by the structured
// node.created/updated/deleted rewrite, so on an unrecognised event kind they
// were silently discarded and the compiled topic was the bare
// `deploy.requested` all along (memql#3614 item 1). The kwargs are gone; the
// compiled topic is byte-identical to what shipped. The concept the payload
// concerns is stated by the writes themselves (createDeployment /
// updateDeploymentStatus on v1:cluster:deployment).
//
// AUTHORIZATION. `authorize` records the pure forward-deploy role decision
// (logic deploymentForwardAllowed, mirroring spec requiresDeveloperOrAbove,
// #1876). The HARD gate is upstream + at the writes: createDeployment /
// updateDeploymentStatus are only ever reached through the Go-gated deploy RPCs
// (deploycontrol.authorize), so the deploy-start write and every transition
// inherit that authorization (specs.memql). The step keeps the decision visible
// + greppable in the run record.
//
// =============================================================================
//   deployEngineCluster -- the one orchestration
// =============================================================================
//
// Sequence (decide -> record -> clone -> build -> place -> gate -> finish):
//
//   authorize       logic deploymentForwardAllowed       (decide: role gate)
//   record          createDeployment                     (persist: one write)
//   clone           action cloneRepoAtVersion            (act: checkout the ref)
//   build           forEach nt: action buildEngineImage  (act: one image / node)
//   importDevImages forEach nt where env==development:    (act: dev placement --
//                     action importImageToK3d              per-node k3d import)
//   place           switch env { development {} default { (act: staging/prod
//                     pinOverlayDigests + argoSync } }     placement -- GitOps)
//   gate            action runDeployGate                 (act: post-deploy gate)
//   outcome         logic deployOutcomeLabel             (decide: succeeded/failed)
//   finalize        switch steps.outcome.result {              (persist + act + persist)
//                     succeeded { updateDeploymentStatus succeeded; notifyDeploy }
//                     default   { revertOverlay; updateDeploymentStatus failed;
//                                 notifyDeploy } }
//
// EXECUTION ORDER. The automation scheduler runs steps in the compiler's
// dependency topo-sort order (a step executes after every step it references).
// The deploy is an ORDERED side-effect pipeline whose phases mostly do NOT pass
// data to each other, so each phase reads only the EVENT payload (a reserved
// root, not a step) and carries no inter-step reference -- which keeps every
// phase at the same dependency depth, where the sort preserves the authored
// source order (clone -> build -> place -> gate). The only data edges are the
// two at the tail: outcome reads gate.result, finalize switches on
// outcome.result, so they sort last exactly where the sequence wants them. A
// forEach/switch step's result is NOT addressable by name, so a later phase
// could not depend on it even if we wanted -- reading from the payload is what
// makes the pipeline order deterministic.
//
// PLACEMENT shape (the env split). `place` is the single env `switch`: the
// development case takes no GitOps action (overlays/ArgoCD are not the dev
// path); staging/production pin the overlay to the resolved digests and ask
// ArgoCD to sync (deploys = merges). The per-NODE development k3d import is the
// adjacent `importDevImages` forEach gated `where env=="development"` -- the
// procedural switch-case grammar hosts single steps, not loops, so a per-node
// loop rides a top-level where-filtered forEach instead of living inside the
// case. Both stay in THIS one automation; there are no per-env automations.
//
// THE SWITCH IS ON `provider`, NOT ON AN ENVIRONMENT (epic memql#3943).
// There is ONE cloud installation and ONE ArgoCD Application, `memql`, so
// there is nothing for an environment to select between. What DOES still
// branch is the deploy TARGET -- a local k3d cluster takes its images by
// import and places nothing, a cloud cluster pins digests and syncs ArgoCD --
// and that distinction has its own field: `provider` (docker-local | azure),
// which `MEMQL_DEPLOY_PROVIDER` sets and `v1:cluster:deployment.provider`
// records. It was always outside the environment gate for exactly this reason.
//
// The alternative -- a logic step mapping provider -> app name -- was rejected
// for a mechanical reason worth recording: every phase here reads ONLY the
// event payload, which is what keeps them all at the same dependency depth
// where the topo-sort preserves authored source order (see EXECUTION ORDER
// above). Making `place` depend on a logic step would give it a data edge the
// other phases do not have, and reorder the pipeline.
//
// The `importImageToK3d(cluster: "memql")` above is NOT the Application name
// wearing the same clothes: that is the k3d CLUSTER name on a developer's
// laptop.
//
// LIFECYCLE record. record -> finalize is the deployment timeline the bundle
// persists: createDeployment (in_progress) at start, updateDeploymentStatus
// (succeeded | failed) at finish -- reconstructable asOf any time off the one
// v1:cluster:deployment concept-id timeline (#1872).
//
// DRY-RUN. The action backends are dry-run-capable capability scripts (I8/I14):
// a docker-local deploy runs end-to-end through the bundle against
// no-op/dry-run backends and records the full lifecycle; the azure path is
// authored + dry-runnable and goes live once the build-server + ArgoCD wiring
// is exercised.

/// Deploy the MemQL ENGINE cluster on the cockpit/runner surface. Sequences clone -> build ->
/// place (provider switch) -> gate -> success/failure finish, recording the deployment lifecycle.
/// Engine mesh ONLY -- no bff or product-carrier nodes.
@trigger(event="deploy.requested")
automation deployEngineCluster {
  args {
    deploymentId         string!
    provider             enum("docker-local", "azure")!
    engineNodeTypes      []string!
    ref                  string!
    version              string!
    workdir              string!
    overlayPath          string
    digests              object
    previousDeploymentId string
    triggeredBy          string
    dryRun               boolean
  }

  step authorize {
    logic deploymentForwardAllowed ( event )
  }
  step record {
    mutation createDeployment (
      deploymentId,
      provider:     provider,
      version,
      triggeredBy:  triggeredBy,
      status:       "in_progress",
      previousDeploymentId
    )
  }
  step clone {
    action cloneRepoAtVersion(workdir, ref: ref, dryRun)
  }
  step build {
    forEach nt in engineNodeTypes {
      action buildEngineImage(nodeType: nt, version: version, workdir, dryRun)
    }
  }
  step importDevImages {
    forEach nt in engineNodeTypes where provider == "docker-local" {
      action importImageToK3d(nodeType: nt, cluster: "memql", version, dryRun)
    }
  }
  step place {
    switch provider {
      case "docker-local" { }
      default {
        action pinOverlayDigests(overlayPath, digests: digests, dryRun)
        action argoSync(app: "memql", revision: ref, dryRun)
      }
    }
  }
  step gate {
    action runDeployGate(provider: provider, workdir: workdir, dryRun)
  }
  step outcome {
    logic deployOutcomeLabel ( passed: steps.gate.result.result.result.passed )
  }
  step finalize {
    switch steps.outcome.result {
      case "succeeded" {
        updateDeploymentStatus { deploymentId: deploymentId, status: "succeeded" }
        action notifyDeploy(status: "succeeded", deploymentId, dryRun)
      }
      default {
        updateDeploymentStatus { deploymentId: deploymentId, status: "failed" }
        action notifyDeploy(status: "failed", deploymentId, dryRun)
      }
    }
  }
  step rollbackTarget {
    logic deployRollbackTarget ( passed: steps.gate.result.result.result.passed, previousDeploymentId )
  }
  step rollback {
    switch steps.rollbackTarget.result {
      case "" { }
      default {
        action revertOverlay(overlayPath, toDeploymentId: steps.rollbackTarget.result, dryRun)
      }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Instance lifecycle -- provision / install / update / repair / deprovision
// =============================================================================
//
// deployEngineCluster above rolls a VERSION onto a cluster that already exists.
// The automations below own the cluster itself, across its whole life
// (epic memql#4463).
//
// WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE SET OF VERBS. The first cloud instance was created by
// hand, so nothing in this tree could recreate it. Its ArgoCD Application then
// pinned a git revision that later vanished from the repository, and at that
// point the running cluster was the ONLY surviving record of the installation:
// unreproducible, and un-auditable. A lifecycle you can only perform by hand is
// a lifecycle nobody can review, test, or repeat -- so each verb below is an
// automation, driven by an event, recorded on the graph.
//
// COMPOSITION. `bringUpInstance` calls `provisionInstance` and then
// `installInstance` as SUB-AUTOMATIONS -- the `automation <name>( ... )` step
// form, the same kind-prefixed invocation shape as `logic <name>( ... )`. That
// keeps each verb independently runnable (an operator repairing an instance
// must not have to run provisioning to get there) while the common path stays
// one event.
//
// WHERE THEY RUN. The cockpit/runner surface, OUTSIDE the target cluster --
// inherited from dsl/deployment/ with no per-action annotation (I13). That is
// not incidental here: a cluster cannot provision itself, and an instance must
// not drive the roll that replaces its own pods. For the FIRST instance the
// runner is a local k3d MemQL (`make up`) or CI; afterwards any instance other
// than the target can drive it.

@trigger(event="instance.provisionRequested")
automation provisionInstance {
  args {
    instanceId     string!
    subscriptionId string!
    resourceGroup  string!
    clusterName    string!
    registryName   string!
    keyVaultName   string!
    location       string
    dryRun         boolean
  }

  step provision {
    action provisionAzureInfrastructure(
      subscriptionId,
      resourceGroup,
      clusterName,
      registryName,
      keyVaultName,
      location,
      dryRun
    )
  }
}

// installInstance OWNS THE ELEVEN STEPS between a provisioned substrate and an
// argoSync that means something (memql#4472). It used to be a one-step alias
// for argoSync -- and argoSync against a freshly provisioned cluster syncs
// NOTHING, because on that cluster there is no ArgoCD, no cert-manager, no
// CNPG, no External Secrets, no ingress controller, no ClusterIssuer, no
// namespace, no credentials and no repository registration.
//
//   1  kubeconfig                       )
//   2  cert-manager                     )
//   3  CNPG operator + Barman plugin    )  installClusterOperators
//   4  ESO CRDs                         )
//   5  External Secrets                 )
//   6  ingress-nginx + IngressClass     )
//   7  letsencrypt-prod ClusterIssuer   )
//   8  namespace + the memql-secrets shell  )  seedInstanceSecrets
//   9  Key Vault secret GENERATION          )
//  10  SecretStore + ExternalSecrets, on THIS instance's identity  ) wireExternalSecrets
//  11  ArgoCD repo credential + AppProject sourceRepos             ) registerGitOpsRepo
//
// and only THEN argoSync, followed by the settle the first sync needs.
//
// THE ORDER IS EXPRESSED AS ONE SWITCH CASE, not as sibling steps, and that is
// deliberate. The scheduler runs steps in dependency topo-sort order, and
// sibling steps that all read only the event payload sit at the same depth
// where the sort preserves authored order -- which is how deployEngineCluster's
// phases stay in sequence. Here the render-diff gate MUST run first and the
// install MUST depend on its verdict, and that data edge would reorder any
// sibling step that lacked one. Putting the whole install inside the gated
// case makes the sequence structural instead of incidental: there is one
// ordering, and it is the one written down.
//
// THE GATE IS THE HIGHEST-VALUE ITEM HERE (memql#4483). `revision` is not a
// scalar to swap. One ?ref= line has cost three retired workarounds, a new
// object needing a host patch, and a new certificate regime needing real Azure
// substrate -- none of it discoverable from the release notes, all of it
// discoverable in about fifteen minutes by rendering both refs. It is OFF for
// a first install (there is no previous ref to diff against) and ON for an
// update, which is what `requireRenderDiff` states.
//
// THE ESO CLIENT ID AND TENANT ARE ARGS, not read out of provisioning's step
// result, because provisionInstance is a SEPARATE VERB by design: an operator
// installing onto substrate that already exists must not have to re-run
// provisioning to obtain them. provisionAzureInfrastructure reports both in its
// result envelope; whoever raises the event carries them across.
@trigger(event="instance.installRequested")
automation installInstance {
  args {
    instanceId        string!
    subscriptionId    string!
    clusterName       string!
    resourceGroup     string!
    overlayPath       string!
    revision          string!
    keyVaultName      string
    tenantId          string
    esoClientId       string
    repoUrl           string
    repoTransport     string
    repoTokenFile     string
    repoProject       string
    acmeEmail         string
    namespace         string
    repoRoot          string
    fromRevision      string
    acceptKinds       string
    requireRenderDiff boolean
    dryRun            boolean
  }

  // BEFORE anything is applied.
  step gate {
    action renderDiff(
      overlayPath,
      toRef:   revision,
      fromRef: fromRevision,
      acceptKinds
    )
  }
  step verdict {
    logic renderDiffVerdict (
      passed:   steps.gate.result.result.result.passed,
      required: requireRenderDiff
    )
  }
  step install {
    switch steps.verdict.result {
      // The gate was required and did not pass. Nothing is applied, and the
      // run record carries the diff -- which is the whole product of this
      // step, since what it found is not discoverable any other way.
      case "blocked" { }
      default {
        action installClusterOperators(
          subscriptionId,
          resourceGroup,
          clusterName,
          repoRoot,
          acmeEmail,
          dryRun
        )
        action seedInstanceSecrets(
          keyVaultName,
          subscriptionId,
          namespace,
          dryRun
        )
        action wireExternalSecrets(
          keyVaultName,
          tenantId,
          esoClientId,
          namespace,
          repoRoot,
          dryRun
        )
        action registerGitOpsRepo(
          repoUrl,
          transport: repoTransport,
          project:   repoProject,
          tokenFile: repoTokenFile,
          dryRun
        )
        action argoSync(app: "memql", revision: revision, dryRun)
        // The first sync legitimately fails while the database initialises,
        // and the mesh then needs a RESTART rather than a retry (memql#4475).
        action settleAfterSync(
          namespace,
          app: "memql",
          dryRun
        )
        // Existence, not health. The whole undeclared-dependency class is
        // invisible to a health check, because the objects were never created.
        action verifyInstallDependencies( namespace )
      }
    }
  }
}

// repairInstance is a CHECK and then a re-sync (memql#4473).
//
// The re-sync is still right for DRIFT: ArgoCD reconciling git IS the
// desired-state engine, so "make the cluster match source again" is the whole
// verb, and anything more -- editing live objects to match an expectation held
// somewhere else -- would reintroduce the second source of truth this epic
// exists to remove.
//
// But an entire class of failure is INVISIBLE to that. The six undeclared
// dependencies were never created, so nothing is unhealthy; Argo reports what
// it applied and has no opinion at all about a CRD nobody installed. A repair
// that only re-syncs therefore DECLARES SUCCESS on a cluster that cannot work,
// which is worse than failing.
//
// So the check comes first, by EXISTENCE, and a missing dependency does NOT
// proceed to the sync. That refusal is the point: syncing cannot create a CRD
// nobody installed, so a sync here would change nothing and report Healthy.
// The run record names what is absent, and installInstance -- which is
// idempotent and safe to re-run -- is what installs it.
@trigger(event="instance.repairRequested")
automation repairInstance {
  args {
    instanceId    string!
    revision      string!
    namespace     string
    ingressClass  string
    clusterIssuer string
    requireIssuer boolean
    overlayPath   string
    productEnv    string
    declaredRef   string
    dryRun        boolean
  }

  // WHAT IS RUNNING, before anything is said about whether it is healthy
  // (memql#4486).
  //
  // A repair report that names one version answers a question nobody asked. The
  // three refs an instance carries are ALLOWED to differ: a tag's image pins are
  // written before that tag's own images exist, so an instance declaring
  // ENGINE_REF=v0.19.6 legitimately runs 0.19.5 binaries. "We are on v0.19.6" is
  // then a statement about MANIFESTS that every reader hears as a statement
  // about CODE -- and during an incident that is the difference between reading
  // the right diff and the wrong one.
  //
  // IT IS A SIBLING OF THE GATE, NOT NESTED UNDER IT, and that is the whole
  // point. The dependency verdict below refuses to sync when something is
  // absent, and a refused repair is EXACTLY when an operator needs to know what
  // is executing. Nesting this under the verdict would withhold the report
  // precisely on the runs that motivate reading one.
  //
  // It reports and never refuses. Divergence here is usually correct, so a step
  // that failed on difference would fail on almost every healthy instance --
  // the same mistake memql#4475 names about reading the first Degraded as
  // failure.
  step version {
    action reportInstanceVersion(
      namespace,
      overlayPath,
      productEnv,
      declaredRef,
      dryRun
    )
  }
  step verify {
    action verifyInstallDependencies(
      namespace,
      ingressClass,
      clusterIssuer,
      requireIssuer
    )
  }
  step verdict {
    logic installDependencyVerdict ( passed: steps.verify.result.result.result.passed )
  }
  step resync {
    switch steps.verdict.result {
      // Deliberately empty. See the header: a sync cannot create what was
      // never installed, and running one anyway is how a broken cluster comes
      // back Healthy.
      case "missing" { }
      default {
        action argoSync(app: "memql", revision: revision, dryRun)
      }
    }
  }
}

// bringUpInstance is provisionInstance then installInstance, and now that
// installInstance means something it is genuinely end to end rather than
// substrate-plus-a-wish (memql#4472).
//
// THE HANDOFF VALUES RIDE THROUGH AS ARGS. tenantId and esoClientId are what
// the instance overlay binds its service accounts to;
// provisionAzureInfrastructure reports esoClientId in its result envelope. They
// are threaded as arguments rather than read out of the substrate step's result
// because each verb has to stay independently runnable -- an operator
// installing onto substrate that already exists must not have to re-run
// provisioning to obtain a value they can read off the vault.
//
// requireRenderDiff is deliberately absent from the pass-through and defaults
// off: a bring-up is a FIRST install, and there is no previous ref to diff
// against. A version bump raises instance.installRequested directly, with the
// gate on.
@trigger(event="instance.bringUpRequested")
automation bringUpInstance {
  args {
    instanceId     string!
    subscriptionId string!
    resourceGroup  string!
    clusterName    string!
    registryName   string!
    keyVaultName   string!
    overlayPath    string!
    revision       string!
    tenantId       string
    esoClientId    string
    repoUrl        string
    repoTransport  string
    repoTokenFile  string
    repoProject    string
    acmeEmail      string
    namespace      string
    repoRoot       string
    location       string
    dryRun         boolean
  }

  step substrate {
    automation provisionInstance(
      instanceId,
      subscriptionId,
      resourceGroup,
      clusterName,
      registryName,
      keyVaultName,
      location,
      dryRun
    )
  }
  step workloads {
    automation installInstance(
      instanceId,
      subscriptionId,
      clusterName,
      resourceGroup,
      overlayPath,
      revision,
      keyVaultName,
      tenantId,
      esoClientId,
      repoUrl,
      repoTransport,
      repoTokenFile,
      repoProject,
      acmeEmail,
      namespace,
      repoRoot,
      dryRun
    )
  }
}

@trigger(event="instance.deprovisionRequested")
automation deprovisionInstance {
  args {
    instanceId           string!
    subscriptionId       string!
    resourceGroup        string!
    confirm              string!
    clusterName          string
    keyVaultName         string
    backupStorageAccount string
    deleteStores         boolean
    purgeKeyVault        boolean
    dryRun               boolean
  }

  // The confirmation rides all the way through as a value rather than being
  // reconstructed here. An orchestrator that could derive its own confirmation
  // from the resource group would be confirming to itself, which is not a
  // safety interlock -- it is a comment.
  step teardown {
    action deprovisionAzureInfrastructure(
      subscriptionId,
      resourceGroup,
      confirm,
      clusterName,
      keyVaultName,
      backupStorageAccount,
      deleteStores,
      purgeKeyVault,
      dryRun
    )
  }
}
